  "reglas": [
    {
      "titulo": "Goal",
      "lineas": [
        "You survive three phases of danger (Dawn, Noon, Dusk) improving your deck of techniques, and then you face the Champions of the Tournament.",
        "How many Champions you face is decided by the difficulty you pick.",
        "You lose if your Energy reaches 0 or less."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "Setup",
      "lineas": [
        "Shuffle the starting combat deck (20 cards).",
        "Separate the three danger decks and pick at random the bosses your level calls for: 2 in this configuration.",
        "You start with 23 Energy in this configuration (healing cap: 23)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "Turn",
      "lineas": [
        "1. Reveal the top danger of the current phase deck.",
        "2. Draw for free up to the danger’s \"free cards\" number. Every extra card costs 1 Energy.",
        "3. Add up the Power of the cards you played and compare it with the danger’s Power.",
        "4. If your total ≥ the danger, you win: the danger card goes to your discard pile as the reward technique.",
        "5. If you lose, subtract the danger’s Damage from your Energy and the danger card leaves the game.",
        "6. Every card you played goes to the discard pile. When the deck runs out, shuffle the discard pile."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "Winning or losing a fight (important)",
      "lineas": [
        "YOU WIN if the sum of your cards ≥ the danger’s Power. The danger card flips over and joins your discard pile as the reward technique: from then on it is one more card in your deck.",
        "YOU LOSE if you stop below the Power. You subtract the danger’s Damage from your Energy and the danger card is discarded out of the game: you do NOT keep it. You never win a card by losing a fight.",
        "Stopping below is not a \"price\" you pay to keep the card: it is giving up. Sometimes it is still worth it, when paying for more draws would cost more Energy than the Damage itself.",
        "Win or lose, every card you played goes to your discard pile."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "How Energy comes back",
      "lineas": [
        "There is no action to heal: you cannot \"rest\" or spend a turn recovering.",
        "Energy only goes up through combat card effects, and those effects fire automatically when the card comes out during a fight. You do not choose when to use them.",
        "Effect \"+X Energy\": applies the moment you draw the card, whether you win or lose afterwards. E.g.: Reflex +1, Discipline +2, Dragon Scale +1, Dragon Fist +2, Serenity +3, Sacred Water +2, Enlightenment +1.",
        "Effect \"+X Energy if you win\": applies only when the fight resolves, and only if you won it. E.g.: Bamboo Fist +1, Crane Wing +1, Crane Flight +2.",
        "You never go over the cap of 23 Energy: the rest is lost.",
        "A design consequence: healing depends on having put healing cards in your deck and on them showing up. That is why it pays to meditate and remove bad cards: a smaller deck makes the good ones come up more often."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "Meditating: taking bad cards out of your deck",
      "lineas": [
        "Meditating is the ONLY way to take cards out of your deck. There is no other.",
        "When: only in the step right after a fight you LOST.",
        "How: pay 1 Energy and remove 1 card(s) from your discard pile. They leave the game for good: they do not come back to the deck.",
        "You can do it several times in a row, paying each time, as long as you have Energy left.",
        "KEY LIMITATION: you can only remove cards that are in the DISCARD PILE. An Existential Doubt still buried in the deck is untouchable: it has to come out in a fight first. That is why the best moment to meditate is right after a fight where your worst cards came out: everything you just played is in the discard pile.",
        "When the deck runs out, the discard pile is shuffled and becomes the deck again: that is when you lose the chance to purge those cards until they come out again.",
        "Why it pays: removing an Existential Doubt (-1) or a Ragged Breath (0) does not raise your total power, but it shrinks the deck and makes the good cards (and the ones that heal Energy) come up more often."
      ]
    },
    {
      "titulo": "Final showdown",
      "lineas": [
        "Reveal the bosses and face them in order, just like a normal danger.",
        "You cannot give up against a boss: as long as you have a card left to draw, you fight it. If you lose, you subtract its Damage and face it again.",
        "You win the run when you beat the last one."
      ]
    }
  ],
